WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.910
- So it looks like we're
gonna learn a lot more about

00:00:01.910 --> 00:00:04.410
why Bill Barr didn't charge
former President Trump

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:06.250
with obstruction of justice.

00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:08.380
Judge Amy Berman Jackson has responded

00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:10.310
to a FOIA lawsuit by CREW,

00:00:10.310 --> 00:00:13.010
the Citizens For Responsible
Ethics in Washington

00:00:13.010 --> 00:00:14.987
by aasically saying that the DOJ more

00:00:14.987 --> 00:00:17.460
or less lied to the court
about its reasonings

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:19.650
for not moving forward with
obstruction of justice.

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:21.720
You may recall that in front of Congress,

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:23.290
former Attorney General Bill Barr said

00:00:23.290 --> 00:00:25.806
that he decided not to
charge former President Trump

00:00:25.806 --> 00:00:27.270
in consultation with the Office

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:29.320
of Legal Counsel and the
department's lawyers.

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:30.500
But according to the court's order,

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:32.740
having reviewed the memo in camera,

00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:33.880
it appears that the DOJ

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:36.525
and Bill Barr decided not to
charge President Trump first

00:00:36.525 --> 00:00:39.114
and then back-filled
everything after the fact.

00:00:39.114 --> 00:00:40.320
And as a result,

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:42.330
we may actually see the reasoning

00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:44.140
and the memo that went into that decision.

00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:45.879
The Biden administration
has 14 days to decide

00:00:45.879 --> 00:00:47.420
whether to file an appeal.

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:48.253
Hopefully they won't.

00:00:48.253 --> 00:00:51.353
So we'll see the memo and
we won't see them in court.

